A||N||3||Seanh||Installing Poly Bushings?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||07-10-2001||07:34 PM||||When I installed my poly bushings on the drivers side front contol arms, I used teflon grease on them to help them slid into the sleeve, and also on the ends so it doesn't squeek. I was just wondering if this was ok? Or should I not have used it for pressing the bushing in? If not, should I remove them and clean 'em, or will it hurt anything. I was just wondering because I never read anything about useing it for this purpose, so I'm just wondering.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||07-10-2001||08:43 PM||||Didn't the bushings come with a tube of grease? When I bought mine they came with a tube of I think silicone grease. It was a clear thick grease. Don't know if teflon grease will react with the poly. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||AkursedX||07-10-2001||08:50 PM||||Well the stuff that comes with the poly is the same stuff that you use for brakes.  It's that sythetic brake grease stuff.  I bought an extra tube and completely covered my bushings in them.<P>Is the teflon stuff brake safe??  If it is, I don't think you;ll have any trouble, but I'm not too sure really<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||07-10-2001||11:23 PM||||Teflon grease or silicone grease should be fine.  My new front sway bar pivot bushings came with a tube of teflon grease, and the fiero store also sells it seperatly for installing poly bushings.<P>Polyurethane is pretty durable stuff.  Most or all "car chemicals" shouldn't bother it.  I assume that you can ruin them with harsh solvents though, like acetone or xylol.<P>So no, you dont want to clean off the grease you put on, you did it just like you were supposed to.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
